            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{A=(1,1),\\;B=(1,2),\\;C=(2,1),\\;D=(1,3),\\;E=(3,1),\\;F=(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le 4\\) (since \\(n=3\\)).  \nWe must cover \\(\\mathcal P\\) with three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Slopes of all unordered pairs\n\nFor each unordered pair \\(\\{X,Y\\}\\subset\\mathcal P\\) we compute the slope \\(m_{XY}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & m_{XY}\\\\ \\hline\nAB & \\infty\\\\\nAC & 0\\\\\nAD & \\infty\\\\\nAE & 0\\\\\nAF & 1\\\\\nBC & -1\\\\\nBD & \\infty\\\\\nBE & -\\tfrac12\\\\\nBF & 0\\\\\nCD & -2\\\\\nCE & 0\\\\\nCF & \\infty\\\\\nDE & -1\\\\\nDF & -1\\\\\nEF & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nA slope is sunny exactly when it is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nHence the **only sunny pairs** are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{A,F\\}},\\qquad\\boxed{\\{B,E\\}},\\qquad\\boxed{\\{C,D\\}} .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other pairs give a non‑sunny slope.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Collinear triples\n\nChecking all triples of \\(\\mathcal P\\) we find that the only collinear triples are\n\n\\[\n\\{A,B,D\\}\\ \\text{on the vertical line }x=1,\\qquad\n\\{A,C,E\\}\\ \\text{on the horizontal line }y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth lines have slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(0\\) respectively, thus are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently **any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)**, and the only possible sunny lines are exactly the three lines determined by the pairs in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Attempt to obtain exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering by three lines exists with exactly two sunny lines.\n\nBecause a sunny line can contain at most two points, the two sunny lines must be two of the three pairs in (2).  \nWithout loss of generality let  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}= \\overline{AF},\\qquad L_{2}= \\overline{BE}.\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover the four points \\(A,F,B,E\\).  \nThe two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) not yet covered are \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \nThe line through them is \\(\\overline{CD}\\); its slope is \\(-2\\), which is sunny.  \nThus the third line \\(L_{3}\\) that must contain \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is also sunny.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to any choice of two sunny pairs: the remaining two points always form the third sunny pair, forcing the third line to be sunny. Hence a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Possibility of a non‑sunny triple line\n\nIf one of the three lines contained three points, it would have to be one of the two non‑sunny triples identified in §2.  \nSuppose we use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (points \\(A,B,D\\)).  \nThe remaining points are \\(C,E,F\\).  \nEvery line determined by any two of these three points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (see the table), i.e. is non‑sunny.  \nThus the remaining two lines would both be non‑sunny, giving **zero** sunny lines, not two.  \nThe same occurs if we start with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n\nTherefore any covering either uses three non‑sunny lines (zero sunny lines) or three sunny lines (three sunny lines), never exactly two.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines that covers all required points and has exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Impossible}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
