ned by a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line). Hence \\(f(3)=3\\).  \n   *\\(m\\ge4\\):* the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\) contains \\(m-1\\) points. Because a sunny line can meet this diagonal in at most one point, at least \\(m-1\\) sunny lines are necessary. On the other hand, \\(m-1\\) sunny lines are sufficient: for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) take  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i- (m-1)}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\) (which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=i+1\\)) and through the point \\((m,\\,m-i)\\) (which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\)).  \n   The slope in (3) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   By construction, for every diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le m\\) the points of that diagonal are distributed one per line, so all points of the triangle are covered.  \n   Hence for every \\(m\\ge4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   f(m)=m-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Feasibility of a given \\(k\\).**  \n   In the reduced problem we have to cover the triangle (2) whose size is \\(m=k+1\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Using (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge f(k+1)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   1 & k=0,1,\\\\[2mm]\n   3 & k=2,\\\\[2mm]\n   k & k\\ge3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality fails only for \\(k=2\\) (because \\(2\\not\\ge3\\)).  \n   For every other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) the inequality holds, and the explicit construction described in \u00a73 (or the trivial constructions for \\(k=0,1\\)) gives the required \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the set (2).  \n   Adding the forced non\u2011sunny diagonals (1) we obtain a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility for \\(k=2\\).**  \n   When \\(k=2\\) the forced non\u2011sunny lines are \\(D_{4},D_{5},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \n   The uncovered points are precisely the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   As observed in step\u202f3, no two of these points can lie on a sunny line; therefore at least three sunny lines are needed, contradicting the requirement that exactly two are sunny.  \n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombining the existence for all \\(k\\neq2\\) with the impossibility for \\(k=2\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor an integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) and is parallel to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Forced non\u2011sunny diagonals\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the conditions and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\) be the set of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nIf a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is **not** a member of \\(\\mathcal N\\), each sunny line can meet \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, because two distinct lines intersect in at most one point.  \nHence at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_s\\) can be covered by the sunny lines.  \nIf \\(D_s\\) contains more than \\(k\\) points, i.e. if \\(s-1>k\\) (equivalently \\(s\\ge k+2\\)), the diagonal must belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\subseteq\\mathcal N\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand there are exactly \\(n-k\\) such diagonals, which matches the number of non\u2011sunny lines we are allowed to use.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The reduced triangle\n\nAfter the diagonals (5) are taken, the only points of \\(S_n\\) that remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(k\\) lines must be sunny and must cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimal number of sunny lines for a right\u2011triangle\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nf(m)=\\min\\{\\text{number of sunny lines covering }\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m=1\\) or \\(m=2\\)* the set consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\); one sunny line suffices, so \\(f(1)=f(2)=1\\).\n\n*If \\(m=3\\)* the set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny two of these points are joined by a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line, all of which are non\u2011sunny; consequently at least three sunny lines are needed and three are enough. Hence \\(f(3)=3\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\ge4\\)* consider the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\); it contains \\(m-1\\) points.  \nA sunny line can intersect this diagonal in at most one point, so at least \\(m-1\\) sunny lines are necessary: \\(f(m)\\ge m-1\\).\n\nTo show that \\(m-1\\) lines are sufficient, for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i-(m-1)}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the points \\((i,1)\\) (lying on the diagonal \\(x+y=i+1\\)) and \\((m,\\,m-i)\\) (lying on the diagonal \\(x+y=m\\)).  \nIts slope is \\(\\dfrac{i}{i-(m-1)}\\); this value is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(1\\le i\\le m-1\\) and \\(m\\ge4\\). Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor a fixed diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le m\\) the points of that diagonal that belong to the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are exactly the points \\((i,\\,s-i)\\) with \\(i=s-1\\); consequently each diagonal contributes **one** point to each line, and every point of the triangle appears on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nThus the \\(m-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\) cover the whole set, proving  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,f(m)=m-1\\qquad(m\\ge4).\\,}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSummarising,\n\n\\[\nf(m)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & m=1,2,\\\\[2mm]\n3 & m=3,\\\\[2mm]\nm-1 & m\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Determination of the admissible \\(k\\)\n\nIn our reduced problem we have to cover the triangle (6), whose size is \\(m=k+1\\), by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTherefore a necessary and sufficient condition is  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge f(k+1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing (9):\n\n* for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) we have \\(f(k+1)=1\\) and (10) holds;\n* for \\(k=2\\) we have \\(f(3)=3\\) and (10) fails;\n* for every \\(k\\ge3\\) we have \\(f(k+1)=k\\) and (10) holds with equality.\n\nConsequently a configuration exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and **does not exist for \\(k=2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Explicit constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\); all are non\u2011sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (non\u2011sunny) and any sunny line through the remaining point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n* **\\(k\\ge3\\).**  \n  \u2013 Include the forced non\u2011sunny diagonals (5).  \n  \u2013 The uncovered triangle has size \\(k+1\\).  \n  \u2013 Apply the construction (7) with \\(m=k+1\\); it yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering that triangle.  \n  The \\(n\\) lines obtained are distinct, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  The forced set (5) is empty; we simply use the \\(n\\) sunny lines given by (7) with \\(m=n+1\\).\n\nAll the above satisfy the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=2\\) the forced non\u2011sunny diagonals are \\(D_4,D_5,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \nThe only points not lying on those diagonals are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line joining two of them is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of these three points can share a sunny line.  \nHence at least three sunny lines are necessary, contradicting the requirement that exactly two are sunny.  \nThus no configuration exists for \\(k=2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\)."
