            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nare distinct and none of them is sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line that can replace a vertical line  \n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=(n-i-1)x+(n-i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.* The slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(n-i-1\\).  \nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), we have \\(n-i-1\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); the line is not vertical, so it is **sunny**.\n\n*Two lattice points on \\(S_i\\).* Substituting \\(x=i\\) and \\(x=i+1\\) in (4) gives\n\n\\[\ny=1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad y=n-i,\n\\]\n\nhence the points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,n-i)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nlie on \\(S_i\\).  \nBoth belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+1+(n-i)=n+1,\\qquad i\\ge1,\\;n-i\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\n*Relation with the vertical lines.* The point \\(Q_i\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{i+1}\\) (or on \\(V_n\\) when \\(i=n\\)).  \n\nConsequently, if we replace the vertical line \\(V_i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) while keeping all other vertical lines, every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remains covered:\n\n* The whole column \\(\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\) is now covered by \\(S_i\\) (it contains the endpoint \\((i,1)\\) and, because its slope is non‑zero, it meets each admissible integer ordinate exactly once).\n* The point \\(Q_i\\) is still covered by the unchanged line \\(V_{i+1}\\); later, when we replace \\(V_{i+1}\\) we will use the same argument, so coverage is never lost.\n\nThus we have proved the following.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (single‑line replacement)\n\n*For any \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the family obtained from \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) by deleting \\(V_i\\) and inserting the sunny line \\(S_i\\) still covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStarting from the all‑vertical family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) we apply Lemma 1 successively to the indices  \n\n\\[\ni=n,\\;n-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-k+1 .\n\\]\n\nAfter the first replacement we have the lines  \n\n\\[\nS_n,\\;V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nAfter the second replacement we have  \n\n\\[\nS_{n-1},S_n,\\;V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-2},\n\\]\n\nand so on.  \nAfter the \\(k\\)-th step the family consists of  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{S_{n-k+1},\\dots ,S_{n}}_{\\displaystyle k\\text{ sunny lines}},\n\\qquad\n\\underbrace{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}}_{\\displaystyle n-k\\text{ non‑sunny lines}} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAt each intermediate stage Lemma 1 guarantees that the covering property holds; therefore it also holds for the final family (6).  \nThe lines are clearly distinct because each \\(S_i\\) contains the point \\((i,1)\\) which no other line of the family contains.\n\nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness  \n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConversely, any admissible configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); obviously \\(k\\) cannot be negative.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
