            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (sunny lines contain at most two points)\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).\nWrite \\(b_i=n+1-a_i-c_i\\) where \\(c_i\\ge0\\).  \nCollinearity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the expressions for \\(b_i\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)+(c_2-c_1)}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{-(a_3-a_2)+(c_3-c_2)}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n-1+\\frac{c_2-c_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =-1+\\frac{c_3-c_2}{a_3-a_2},\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{c_2-c_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{c_3-c_2}{a_3-a_2}=t\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=-1+t .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(t=0\\) then \\(m=-1\\) and \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIf \\(t>0\\) then \\(m>-1\\). Because the numerator and denominator in the\ndefinition of \\(t\\) are integers, \\(t\\) is a rational number; the only\npossibilities for a line through lattice points with integer slope are\n\\(t=0,1\\). Thus \\(m\\in\\{-1,0\\}\\).  \n\\(m=0\\) gives a horizontal line, and the case of an undefined slope\ncorresponds to a vertical line. All three possibilities are excluded by\nthe definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration uses at most one non‑sunny line.\nA non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\);\nin any case it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n(because the largest column or row of the triangle has length \\(n\\)).\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most two points.\nHence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+2\\,(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\( |\\mathcal P_n|=n(n+1)/2>2n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\nThus at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nDefine, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-(n-i-1)\\,x+(n-i).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-(n-i-1)\\); because \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) and\n\\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(-(n-i-1)\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical,\nhence \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFrom (2) we obtain the two lattice points\n\n\\[\n(i,1)\\in L_i,\\qquad (i+1,n-i-1)\\in L_i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because \\(i+1+(n-i-1)=n\\) and the\ncoordinates are positive.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_{n-1}:\\;x=n-1,\\qquad V_{n}:\\;x=n\n\\]\n\nbe two vertical (non‑sunny) lines.  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{L_i\\mid i\\in S\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus S\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{V_{n-1},V_n\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) has cardinality \\(|S|=k\\).\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(n-2-k\\) vertical\nlines, and the two fixed vertical lines \\(V_{n-1},V_n\\); altogether \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.\n\nWe prove that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Coverage of columns belonging to vertical lines.*  \nIf a column \\(x=i\\) is represented by a vertical line (i.e. \\(i\\notin S\\)),\nall points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) lie on that line.\n\n*Coverage of columns replaced by a sunny line.*  \nLet \\(i\\in S\\).  \nThe point \\((i,1)\\) is on \\(L_i\\) by (3).  \nFor any other admissible point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) we have\n\\(b\\le n+1-i\\), hence \\(b-1\\le n-i-1\\).  \nConsequently the point \\((i+1,b-1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and,\nby construction, lies on the vertical line \\(x=i+1\\) (which is present\nbecause either \\(i+1\\notin S\\) or, if \\(i+1\\in S\\), the same argument\napplies recursively).  \nSince the slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-(n-i-1)\\), the line through\n\\((i+1,b-1)\\) with that slope passes exactly through \\((i,b)\\); thus\n\\((i,b)\\) is also on \\(L_i\\).  \nHence every point of column \\(i\\) is covered by \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Coverage of the two rightmost columns.*  \nThe columns \\(x=n-1\\) and \\(x=n\\) are covered by the vertical lines\n\\(V_{n-1}\\) and \\(V_n\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  The construction works for any \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\n- Lemma 1 forces at least two lines to be non‑sunny, so \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n- The explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) shows that every integer\n  \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\; } .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
