s **not guaranteed** by the given information. The proof contains a logical fallacy (a misapplication of the pigeonhole principle).  \n  **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the statement is false in general and the argument is invalid.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (three uncovered points)\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cDefine the three points  \n  \\[\n  P_1=(a,b),\\qquad\n  P_2=(a,\\,c-a),\\qquad\n  P_3=(c-b,\\,b),\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n  where \\(a,b,c\\) are as in Lemma\u202f1.\n\n  *All three points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*  \n  From (5) we have \\(a+b\\le n\\le n+1\\), so \\(P_1\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n  Because \\(c\\le a+b\\), we have \\(c-a\\le b\\) and \\(c-b\\le a\\); together with \\(c>a\\) and \\(c>b\\) (otherwise \\(c\\) would not be the smallest missing anti\u2011diagonal sum) this yields \\(1\\le c-a\\le b\\) and \\(1\\le c-b\\le a\\). Hence \\(P_2,P_3\\) also satisfy the inequalities defining \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\u201d\n\n  **Issue 1 \u2013 Use of \\(c>a\\) and \\(c>b\\):**  \n  The solution asserts \u201c\\(c>a\\) and \\(c>b\\)\u201d without justification. As noted in Lemma\u202f1, \\(c\\) can be smaller than \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) (e.g., \\(a=3,b=3,c=2\\)). Hence these inequalities are **unproved and can be false**.  \n  **Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n  **Issue 2 \u2013 Dependence on the false inequality \\(c\\le a+b\\):**  \n  The claim that \\(c-a\\le b\\) and \\(c-b\\le a\\) follows from \\(c\\le a+b\\). Since the latter inequality is not established (and is false in general), the derived bounds on the coordinates of \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) are unsupported. Consequently the statement \u201cAll three points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\u201d lacks a valid justification.  \n  **Classification:** **Justification Gap** (the conclusion may be true in some cases, but the argument is insufficient).\n\n- **Quote:** \u201c*None of the three points lies on a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).*  \n\n  - \\(P_1\\) has \\(x=a\\) and \\(y