esp. \\(h,d\\)) be the number of missing vertical (resp. horizontal, diagonal) lines.  \n   By definition \\(v\\ge n-a+1\\) and \\(h\\ge n-b+1\\).  \n   Since the total number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(n-2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (n-v)+(n-h)+(n-d)\\le n-2\\Longrightarrow v+h+d\\ge 2n+2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the lower bounds for \\(v\\) and \\(h\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (n-a+1)+(n-b+1)+d\\ge 2n+2\\Longrightarrow d\\ge a+b .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The diagonal lines are indexed by the sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); if at least \\(a+b\\) of them are missing, then among the first \\(a+b\\) indices at least one must be missing. Consequently the smallest missing index \\(c\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   c\\le a+b+1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (5) with the fact that the smallest missing index cannot exceed the number of missing lines gives (3). \u220e\n\n   *Lemma\u202f2 (three points not hit by any non\u2011sunny line).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(a,b),\\qquad\n   P_2=(a,\\,c-a),\\qquad\n   P_3=(c-b,\\,b).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Then each \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and none of them lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that is non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Because \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) and \\(c\\le a+b+1\\) (Lemma\u202f1), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le c-a\\le b+1,\\qquad 1\\le c-b\\le a+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(c-a)=c\\le a+b+1\\le n+1,\\qquad\n   (c-b)+b=c\\le n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   so all three points satisfy the defining inequalities of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Why they are not on a present non\u2011sunny line.*  \n\n   \u2013 The vertical line \\(x=a\\) is missing, so \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) are not on any vertical line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n   \u2013 The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is missing, so \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\) are not on any horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n   \u2013 The diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) is missing, so \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) are not on any diagonal line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   Hence none of the three points belongs to a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\). \u220e\n\n   *Lemma\u202f3 (a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points).*  \n   The three points form an \u201cL\u2019\u2019 shape:\n\n   \\[\n   P_1P_2\\text{ is vertical},\\qquad\n   P_1P_3\\text{ is horizontal},\\qquad\n   P_2P_3\\text{ has slope }-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore any line that contains two of them is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); such a line is non\u2011sunny. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).\n\n   *Conclusion.*  \n   The three points of Lemma\u202f2 are not covered by any non\u2011sunny line, and by Lemma\u202f3 each of them requires a distinct sunny line. Hence any covering family must contain at least three sunny lines. This contradicts the assumption that \\(\\mathcal F\\) has only two (or any number \\(k\\ge 2\\)) sunny lines. Therefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The explicit constructions of \u00a7\u202f2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   The impossibility proof of \u00a7\u202f3 shows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) can exist.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]"
