.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 2.**  \n   Each of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and none of them lies on a non‑sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Because of (4) we have \\(c\\le a+b\\).  Together with \\(c\\ge 2\\) this implies  \n\n   \\[\n   c-a\\ge 1,\\qquad c-b\\ge 1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(c-a)=c\\le a+b\\le n,\\qquad (c-b)+b=c\\le n .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence all three points satisfy the defining inequalities of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   By construction the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is missing, so \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) are not on any present vertical line.  \n   The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is missing, so \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\) are not on any present horizontal line.  \n   The diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) is missing, so \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) are not on any present diagonal line.  \n   Consequently none of the three points lies on a non‑sunny line belonging to \\(\\mathcal F\\). ∎\n\n   *3.4  A sunny line can contain at most one of the three points.*\n\n   **Lemma 3.**  \n   Any line that contains two of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is non‑sunny.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The segment \\(P_1P_2\\) is vertical (both have \\(x=a\\)).  \n   The segment \\(P_1P_3\\) is horizontal (both have \\(y=b\\)).  \n   The segment \\(P_2P_3\\) satisfies \\((c-a)+(c-b)=2c-(a+b)=c\\) and \\((c-a)-(c-b)=b-a\\); a direct computation shows its slope is \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence any line through two of the points belongs to one of the three non‑sunny families. ∎\n\n   *3.5  Deriving a contradiction.*  \n   By Lemma 2 the three points are uncovered by non‑sunny lines, and by Lemma 3 a sunny line can cover **at most one** of them.  Therefore at least three distinct sunny lines are required to cover the three points, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains only two (or any number \\(k\\ge 2\\)) sunny lines.  Hence a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The explicit constructions of § 2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   The impossibility proof of § 3 shows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) can exist.  \n\nThus the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
