1sunny lines are missing; consequently each of the three families misses at least one line, so the numbers \\(a,b,c\\) are well defined and satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a,b\\le n,\\qquad 2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *3.2  A quantitative relation between \\(a,b,c\\).*\n\n   **Lemma 1.**  \n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\(V,H,D\\) denote the numbers of **present** vertical, horizontal and diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) lines, respectively.  \n   By definition of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\),\n\n   \\[\n   V\\le a-1,\\qquad H\\le b-1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the total number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(n-2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   V+H+D\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The diagonal lines are indexed by the sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   Because \\(c\\) is the smallest missing diagonal, all diagonals with index \\(<c\\) are present; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   D\\le c-2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (5) and (7) into (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (a-1)+(b-1)+(c-2)\\le n-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a+b+c\\le n+2 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(c\\ge 2\\), inequality (8) yields \\(a+b\\le n\\), which is (4). \u220e\n\n   *3.3  Three points that are not hit by any non\u2011sunny line.*\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(a,b),\\qquad\n   P_2=(a,\\,c-a),\\qquad\n   P_3=(c-b,\\,b).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 2.**  \n   Each of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and none of them lies on a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Because of (4) we have \\(c\\le a+b\\).  Together with \\(c\\ge 2\\) this implies  \n\n   \\[\n   c-a\\ge 1,\\qquad c-b\\ge 1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(c-a)=c\\le a+b\\le n,\\qquad (c-b)+b=c\\le n .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence all three points satisfy the defining inequalities of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   By construction the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is missing, so \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\)